    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="0650" n="546" sameAs="frus:frus1933v02/pg546"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.51 War Credits/641</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Greek Minister (Simopoulos) to the Acting Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2145</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1933-11-11">November 11,
                        1933</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: In reply to your letter dated
                    October 31, 1933, by which you kindly transmitted the communication of the
                    Acting Secretary of Treasury relative to my country’s debt to the United States,
                    I have the honor to inform you that, while the Greek Government considers the
                    second loan under the Agreement of May 10, 1929, as a war loan, taking, <pb
                        facs="0651" n="547" xml:id="pg547"/> however, into consideration the
                    objections of the United States Government on this matter and desiring to avoid
                    any discussions, it would be disposed to grant to this debt the same treatment
                    accorded last year,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d381-f1"/> expressly maintaining, as
                    in the past, its point of view on the character of this debt.<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d381-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Consequently, the Greek Government will recognize against the interest of this
                    loan due May 10, 1933 and November 10, 1933, the same percentage which will be
                    applied for the stabilization loan.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">C. Simopoulos</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
